First Passage Time Formula for Single Vertex Graphs
\label{ssect1.3}
In this subsection we set up some additional notation which will be used throughout
this article. Also we record a special form of the well-known first passage time formula,
e.g., \cite{Ra56,ItMc74}.

Let $X$ be a Brownian motion on $\mathcal{G}$ in the sense of definition~\ref{def_BM}
defined on a family $\bigl(\Omega,\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{F}=(\mathcal{F}_t,\,t\ge 0), (P_\xi,\,\xi\in\mathcal{G})\bigr)$
of filtered probability spaces. Let $H_v$ be the hitting time of the vertex $v$.
It follows from definition~\ref{def_BM} that for all $\xi\in\mathcal{G}$, $P_\xi(H_v<+\infty)=1$.
For $\lambda>0$, set
\begin{equation}    \label{eq1.14}
    e_\lambda(\xi) = E_\xi\bigl(\exp(-\lambda H_v)\bigr) = e^{-\ensuremath\sqrt{2\gl} d(\xi,v)},\qquad \xi\in\mathcal{G},
\end{equation}
where $E_\xi(\,\cdot\,)$ denotes expectation with respect to $P_\xi$. The last
equality follows from formula~\eqref{eq1.5}.

Recall that we denote the natural metric on $\mathcal{G}$ by $d$. We introduce another
symmetric map $d_v$ from $\mathcal{G}\times\mathcal{G}$ to $\mathbb{R}_+$ defined by
\begin{equation}    \label{eq1.15}
    d_v(\xi,\eta) = d(\xi,v) + d(v,\eta),\qquad \xi,\,\eta\in\mathcal{G},
\end{equation}
which is the ``distance from $\xi$ to $\eta$ via the vertex $v$''. Observe that if
$\xi$, $\eta\in\mathcal{G}$ do not belong to the same edge, then $d_v(\xi,\eta)=d(\xi,\eta)$ holds.

Next we define two heat kernels on $\mathcal{G}$ by
\begin{align}
    p(t,\xi,\eta)   &= \sum_{k=1}^n \onel(\xi)\, g\bigl(t,d(\xi,\eta)\bigr)\,\onel(\eta),
                    \label{eq1.16}\\
    p_v(t,\xi,\eta) &= \sum_{k=1}^n \onel(\xi)\, g\bigl(t,d_v(\xi,\eta)\bigr)\,\onel(\eta),
                    \label{eq1.17}
\end{align}
with $t>0$, $\xi$, $\eta\in\mathcal{G}$. $g$ is the Gau{\ss}-kernel defined in
equation~\eqref{eq1.4}. Hence, in local coordinates $\xi=(k,x)$, $\eta=(m,y)$, $x$, $y\ge
0$, $k$, $m\in\ensuremath\{1,2,\dotsc,n\}$, these kernels read
\begin{align}
    p\bigl(t,(k,x),(m,y)\bigr)
        &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi t}}\,e^{-(x-y)^2/2t}\,\delta_{km}\label{eq1.18}\\
    p_v\bigl(t,(k,x),(m,y)\bigr)
        &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi t}}\,e^{-(x+y)^2/2t}\,\delta_{km}\label{eq1.19}.
\end{align}
The \emph{Dirichlet heat kernel} $p^D$ on $\mathcal{G}$ is then given by
\begin{equation}    \label{eq1.20}
    p^D(t,\xi,\eta) = p(t,\xi,\eta) - p_v(t,\xi,\eta),\qquad t>0,\,\xi,\,\eta\in\mathcal{G}.
\end{equation}
It is the transition density of a strong Markov process with state space
$\mathcal{G}\ensuremath^\circ\cup\{\Delta\}$ which on every edge of $\mathcal{G}\ensuremath^\circ$ is equivalent to a Brownian
motion until the moment of reaching the vertex when it is
killed, and $\Delta$ denotes a universal cemetery state for all stochastic processes
considered adjoined to $\mathcal{G}$ as an isolated point. (Observe that this process is
\emph{not} a Brownian motion on $\mathcal{G}$ in the sense of
definition~\ref{def_BM}.)

The \emph{Dirichlet resolvent} $R^D = (R^D_\lambda,\,\lambda>0)$ on $\mathcal{G}$ is defined by
\begin{equation}    \label{eq1.21}
    R^D_\lambda f(\xi)
        = E_\xi\Bigl(\int_0^{H_v} e^{-\lambda t} f(X_t)\,dt\Bigr),\qquad
            \lambda>0,\,\xi\in\mathcal{G},\,f\in B(\mathcal{G}).
\end{equation}
It is easy to see that $R^D_\lambda$ has the following integral kernel on $\mathcal{G}$
\begin{equation}    \label{eq1.22}
    r^D_\lambda(\xi,\eta) = r_\lambda(\xi,\eta) - r_{v,\lambda}(\xi,\eta),\qquad \xi,\,\eta\in\mathcal{G},
\end{equation}
where for $\xi$, $\eta\in\mathcal{G}$,
\begin{equation}    \label{eq1.22a}
    r_\lambda(\xi,\eta) = \sum_{k=1}^n \onel(\xi)\,\frac{e^{-\ensuremath\sqrt{2\gl}\,d(\xi,\eta)}}{\ensuremath\sqrt{2\gl}}\,\onel(\eta),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}    \label{eq1.22b}
\begin{split}
    r_{v,\lambda}(\xi,\eta)
        &= \sum_{k=1}^n \onel(\xi)\, \frac{e^{-\ensuremath\sqrt{2\gl}\, d_v(\xi,\eta)}}{\ensuremath\sqrt{2\gl}}\,\onel(\eta)\\[1ex]
        &= \sum_{k=1}^n \onel(\xi)\, \frac{1}{\ensuremath\sqrt{2\gl}}\,e_\lambda(\xi)\, e_\lambda(\eta)\,\onel(\eta).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
In particular, $r^D_\lambda$ is the Laplace transform of the Dirichlet heat
kernel~\eqref{eq1.20} at $\lambda>0$.

\vspace{.5\baselineskip}
In the present context the well-known first passage time formula, e.g., \cite{Ra56, ItMc74},
reads as follows
\begin{equation*}
    R_\lambda f(\xi) = E_\xi\Bigl(\int_0^S e^{-\lambda t} f(X_t)\,dt\Bigr)
                    + E_\xi\bigl(e^{-\lambda S}\,R_\lambda f(X_S)\bigr),
\end{equation*}
where $S$ is any $P_\xi$--a.s.\ finite stopping time relative to $\mathcal{J}$.
The choice $S=H_v$ gives the following result.

\begin{lemma}   \label{lem1.4}
Let $X$ be a Brownian motion on $\mathcal{G}$ with resolvent $R=(R_\lambda,\,\lambda>0)$. Then
for all $\lambda>0$, $\xi\in\mathcal{G}$, $f\in B(\mathcal{G})$,
\begin{equation}    \label{eq1.23}
    R_\lambda f(\xi) = R^D_\lambda f(\xi) + e_\lambda(\xi)\,R_\lambda f(v)
\end{equation}
holds true.
\end{lemma}

The following notation will be convenient. For real valued measurable functions
$f$, $g$ on $\mathcal{G}$, with restrictions $f_k$, $g_k$, $k\in\ensuremath\{1,2,\dotsc,n\}$, to the edges
